   monotone decreasing along the path: True

C. The mechanism test
   energy drop BEFORE the junction ever touches a face:
     E*(60°) − E*(→120°) = +0.117280 q̃²
   A junction-contact penalty δ acts only at θ ≥ 120°. Even δ → ∞
   (an impenetrable wall at contact) leaves the minimum at the 120°
   isosceles, +0.1178 q̃² BELOW the equilateral ring.
   The equilateral point is a one-sided cubic inflection along this
   path — it is never the minimum for ANY δ ∈ [0, ∞].

D. Size vs mechanism, and the layer's own expansion parameter
   penalty size needed to beat the chain: 0.2616 q̃² = 0.291 m_e  (natural core ~ √σ = m_e: available)
   BUT mechanism fails regardless of size (part C).
   thin-string expansion parameter at the operating point:
     w/d = (w·√σ)/c₂ = 0.8–1.1 / 1.9236 = 0.42–0.57
   The faces sit ~2 string-widths apart: the classical thin-string
   layer — the three-term balance AND this instability analysis —
   is uncontrolled at O(1) here.

VERDICT
  1. Candidate (a), junction-core energetics: FAILS at the thin-string
     layer. The needed size is natural, but the mechanism cannot work:
     a contact effect cannot cure an interior instability, and the
     energy is already 0.118 q̃² below the ring before contact.
  2. The stability debt escalates: at w/d ≈ 0.5 the ring-vs-chain
     question is genuinely undecidable at the classical layer. It is
     a beyond-thin-string question — the geometry of the three-source
     ground state (Y-junction-like vs collinear) is lattice-computable
     in the same SU(2) N_f = 3 campaign that referees T_c/√σ, F_π/√σ,
     and w·√σ.
  3. Candidates (b) zero-point of the soft pair and (c) color-structure
     rigidity remain open — and (b) is strengthened: soft modes at
     w/d ≈ 0.5 have O(1) quantum corrections.
  4. Nothing promoted; ledger follow-up owed on (a).
